# Cloudflare Workers static-asset headers.
# Copied to the output root by the `assets` entry in angular.json.

# E-44 (2026-08-20): security headers, applied to every path. The release-gating
# table (Private Roadmap.md) flags these as unreviewed - "nothing in this
# document set records these ever having been reviewed" - which was simply true
# until now.
#
# Four are enforcing immediately: they're well-understood and, critically,
# cannot break anything this app actually does, because none of them restrict
# WHERE the app is allowed to load scripts/styles/images/fetches FROM - they
# only harden things (HSTS, no-sniff, no-framing, referrer trimming) that no
# legitimate RangerTrak behaviour depends on.
#
# The Content-Security-Policy is now ENFORCING (2026-09-22). It spent a month
# as Report-Only, which was the right call and did its job - see below for the
# one real violation that surfaced in that time.
#
# Two things had to be true before flipping it, and now both are:
#
# 1. A violation actually showed up and was fixed. PageSpeed Insights
#    (2026-09-22) logged a CSP issue against Cloudflare's own Web Analytics
#    beacon, which the edge injects into the page - `script-src 'self'` would
#    have blocked it. static.cloudflareinsights.com is now allowed explicitly.
#    That is precisely the class of silent breakage Report-Only existed to
#    catch, and it would have shipped unnoticed otherwise.
#
# 2. The policy is testable locally. tools/serve-dist.js now reads and applies
#    this file (it previously ignored it, which is why this comment used to say
#    the policy was "genuinely untested until it reaches the live edge"). The
#    e2e suite runs against that server, so an enforcing CSP that breaks map
#    tiles or geocoding now fails locally instead of during a callout.
#
# Also simplified: fonts.googleapis.com and fonts.gstatic.com are GONE from the
# policy, because the webfonts are self-hosted as of the same date. Two fewer
# third-party origins to trust.
#
# ONE BUILD SETTING IS LOAD-BEARING FOR THIS: angular.json's
# `optimization.styles.inlineCritical` must stay FALSE. With it on, Angular
# emits the compiled stylesheet as
#     <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles-*.css" media="print" onload="this.media='all'">
# and an enforcing script-src blocks inline EVENT HANDLERS. A hash cannot cover
# one without 'unsafe-hashes' (same constraint that moved theme-init.js out of
# index.html). The stylesheet then never activates, the app renders unstyled,
# and /map overflows a phone viewport. That is exactly what happened on the
# first enforcing build, and the e2e suite caught it - because serve-dist.js
# now applies this file. Turning inlineCritical back on means going back to
# Report-Only, or finding another way to defer that stylesheet.
#
# If something does break in the field, the fast rollback is to rename this
# header back to Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only and redeploy - that
# restores the previous logging-only behaviour without changing the policy.
#
# No Strict-Transport-Security line here - confirmed live 2026-08-20 (curl
# against the deployed edge, twice, cache bypassed both times) that the zone
# already has Cloudflare's own dashboard-level HSTS enabled
# (SSL/TLS -> Edge Certificates -> HTTP Strict Transport Security), serving
# `max-age=15552000; includeSubDomains; preload` - which INCLUDES preload, a
# stronger commitment than this file was asking for, and which Cloudflare's
# zone-level feature serves regardless of what an origin header says. An origin
# HSTS declaration here would have been the exact "looks configured, does
# nothing" trap this project keeps finding (ADR D-39's catalogue) - it's not
# wrong, it's just never read. tools/check-deployed.js verifies the real
# zone-level value instead of a redundant origin declaration.
#
# Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy and Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy added 2026-08-26
# (E-44 audit, securityheaders.com pass) - both enforcing immediately, same
# "cannot break anything this app does" reasoning as the original four. COOP
# isolates this page's browsing context group from cross-origin openers/
# openees (no window.open() popups, no OAuth-style flows here). CORP:
# same-origin governs whether OTHER sites can embed RangerTrak's own JS/CSS/
# images - it has zero effect on RangerTrak's own outbound fetches to OSM/
# Nominatim/Fonts, and nothing legitimately needs to embed this app's assets
# cross-origin.
#
# connect-src also carries fonts.gstatic.com (2026-09-01) - not because the font fetch
# itself needs it (font-src already covers that, and a live CDP script confirmed the actual
# byte fetch is a single, normal, parser-initiated request that succeeds without it), but
# because Chrome checks a font-display:swap font's internal load-tracking against connect-src
# rather than font-src - a Blink quirk, not an app bug. Same reasoning as opentopomap needing
# both img-src and connect-src below (leaflet.offline's fetch()-based caching), just for a
# different underlying mechanism.
#
# Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy is deliberately Report-Only, NOT enforcing,
# same reasoning as the CSP above and for a concretely checked reason: COEP's
# strict `require-corp` mode would very likely break the map tiles outright -
# confirmed live 2026-08-26 that OpenStreetMap's tile servers and Nominatim
# send no `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy` header, and Leaflet/MapLibre load
# tiles as plain no-cors `<img>`/tile requests, which `require-corp` blocks
# without one (their `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` doesn't help - that only
# matters for CORS-mode requests, not no-cors ones). `credentialless` mode
# was chosen instead of `require-corp` because it doesn't require a CORP
# header at all - it only strips credentials from cross-origin requests, and
# none of this app's cross-origin fetches (OSM tiles, Nominatim, Google
# Fonts, optionally Google Geocoding) ever send credentials to begin with -
# so enforcing it should be safe once observed, but "should be" is exactly
# why this is Report-Only first rather than assumed.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  # geolocation=(self), not (): an empty allowlist disables the API for EVERY origin,
  # including this one, so E-114 Phase 1a's field-mode GPS auto-fill
  # (entry.component.ts tryGpsAutoFill()) could never get a fix on the deployed site - it
  # failed silently to the mission default location, because the code's
  # `!navigator.geolocation` guard passes (the object exists; the CALL is what's rejected)
  # and the error callback only logs a warning. Invisible to tools/e2e.js, which runs
  # against localhost where no header applies. Corrected 2026-08-31.
  # The other four stay disabled: this app calls no camera, microphone, USB or payment
  # API anywhere (photo import is a plain <input type="file">, which needs no grant), so
  # they cost nothing and are kept as defence in depth.
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(self), camera=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy-Report-Only: credentialless
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://static.cloudflareinsights.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; font-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data: blob: https://*.tile.openstreetmap.org https://*.tile.opentopomap.org https://server.arcgisonline.com; connect-src 'self' https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org https://maps.googleapis.com https://api.what3words.com https://*.tile.openstreetmap.org https://*.tile.opentopomap.org https://cloudflareinsights.com; worker-src 'self'; manifest-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'

# Only the files that MUST NOT be cached are listed below. Everything else keeps
# Cloudflare's default ETag revalidation, which is already correct for the
# hash-named bundles (main-XXXX.js, chunk-XXXX.js, styles-XXXX.css) since a
# new build produces a new filename.
#
# These four are the service-worker control plane. If any of them is served
# stale, browsers keep running the previous release forever — the exact
# failure that masked the dead IONOS origin from existing users in 2026-08.

/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/ngsw.json
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/ngsw-worker.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/safety-worker.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/manifest.webmanifest
  Cache-Control: no-cache
